Z||000000||firstfiero||04-08-2001||08:48 PM||||     I hate driving my car at night because the lights are just so dim. Even the high beams aren't that good.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/mad.gif"> Is their any easy upgrade for this? Maybe a better kind of light...<P>------------------<BR>Hey Mr. Ricer come over here and tell me how your stickers make you faster.....<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/headshot.gif"> <BR>Todd <BR>  86se||||reg||9
Z||000001||85Josh||04-08-2001||09:10 PM||||Get yourself a multimeter and test the voltage at the headlight itself. Do this both with the engine off and on. It should test at least 12 volts off, and at least 13 on. If you have lower voltages, then you have a loose or corroded connection somewhere. Good Luck hunting bad wires!<P>------------------<BR>85 2M4(Lil' Bit)||||reg||1
Z||000002||FieroJoe||04-08-2001||09:29 PM||||If you have enough voltage to the lights, then possibly your headlights just plain suck.  My friends truck (also 6054 lights) had some crappy lights in it, his MagLite was damn near brighter.  He just got a new set and everything was good.  Just go to your local autoparts place and buy a new set.  Phillips make some good ones, and you might want to spend the extra cash and try some of the ones that are the extra bright ones.  Just make sure you dont buy the same brand that you had!<P>Also, for a mod, there are a lot of kits now that convert the sealed beam units to a replaceable Hella H4 bulb.  <A HREF="http://www.AutoOptiks.com" TARGET=_blank>www.AutoOptiks.com</A>  has a set.  The Fiero Store does also, but I have heard the workmanship of the units is questionable.<P>------------------<BR>AIM: Snowboarder0311<BR>87 Coupe 5sp. - big resto in progress<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/profile_1.JPG">  <p>[This message has been edited by FieroJoe (edited 04-08-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||Taijiguy||04-08-2001||10:41 PM||||Ferget those lameassed Sylvania's....lay out the cash for a set of CIBIE's or Hella's. Then order a set of 80/150 bulbs for them. Don't leave those bad boys pointed at your garage door too long, you'll set the sucker on fire!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000004||firstfiero||04-09-2001||06:44 AM||||   Thanks guys...I don't think it's a  voltage problem. I think the headlights just suck..  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Hey Mr. Ricer come over here and tell me how your stickers make you faster.....<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/headshot.gif"> <BR>Todd <BR>  86se||||reg||14
Z||000005||Carrolles||04-09-2001||11:01 AM||||I got a set of the higher output lights from WalMart adn there was quite a difference.  Not bad for $19.00/pair.||||reg||1
Z||000006||rodmcneill||04-09-2001||11:50 AM||||I replaced the headlights in both of my GT's with the high output ones from WalMart. The low beam goes from a stock 35 watts to a 55 watt output, the highbeam stays the same at 65 watts. Bulbs do wear out over time,less light output and also if you look inside the reflector of the bulb you will see corrosion of the chrome finish. New bulbs made a big difference for night time driving.||||reg||1
Z||000007||theogre||04-09-2001||07:40 PM||||A bit of warning.... higher wattage headlights are very often illegal for street use.<P>Read my cave article about headlights and aim yours. If the aim is off then you'll have poor lighting from any light on it.<P>Check the voltage at the lights even if you think it's ok. Also check the wire to ground. Either bad will cause dim lights.<P>I like the Philips High Visibility in the cave article. Fully street legal but they provide a better lighting patern, especially on low beam, than most lights. (Including allot of the higher wattage jobs.)||||reg||1
